  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:DtCqblM6Syg",
    "purpose": "Show how to cook pan-seared, butter-basted tenderloin steaks with a mushroom pan sauce. It also compares, side by side, a steak salted and left uncovered in the fridge overnight with one salted just before cooking.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Salting a steak well ahead of time and resting it uncovered in the fridge overnight gives a drier surface for a better sear and a juicier, more tender interior. A side-by-side cook of two tenderloin steaks demonstrates this.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Two tenderloin steaks cut from the same tenderloin are cooked identically. The only difference is that one was salted and rested uncovered overnight and the other was salted just before cooking.",
      "The video explains why surface moisture slows browning: the latent heat of vaporization and the Maillard reaction.",
      "Tenderloin is basted with butter, shallots and thyme, and pulled before its target temperature to allow for carryover cooking.",
      "A mushroom pan sauce is built with stock, soy sauce, mustard and butter, then finished with acid.",
      "The steaks are cut and tasted to compare crust, juice retention and texture, with a mention of a steak salted for 4 days."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Tenderloin prep and science",
        "summary": "Introduces the two tenderloin steaks, one salted and rested uncovered overnight and one unsalted. Explains what salting ahead and leaving the steak uncovered do, and seasons with pepper ground in a mortar and pestle.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Searing the steaks",
        "summary": "Both steaks go into a hot pan with olive oil, and the difference in sizzle is used to explain evaporation energy, latent heat and the Maillard reaction. Compares the crusts after flipping, using chef's presses, and covers preferred doneness for lean versus fatty cuts.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Basting and cooking",
        "summary": "Adds shallots, thyme and butter and bastes the steaks, with tips for basting on induction versus gas. Checks temperatures, explains carryover cooking, sears the edges, and rests the steaks under the basting fat.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Making the mushroom sauce",
        "summary": "Browns mixed mushrooms with butter, oil, salt, MSG and pepper, then softens shallots with thyme. Adds soy sauce, whole grain mustard, chicken stock and butter and reduces the sauce to an emulsified, creamy texture.",
        "first_segment": 14,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "Results and taste test",
        "summary": "Slices the salted and unsalted steaks to compare crust and juice retention, and mentions a steak salted for 4 days that turned ham-like. Finishes the sauce with yuzu juice, plates both steaks, and tastes them.",
        "first_segment": 17,
        "last_segment": 24
      }
    ],
